                        "review": "I've only scratched the surface. It's not terrible, if you have some time for a bit of grind, as takes quite some time in one round to do all that's possible. There's a lot of management and missing QOL features, but it works for the most part. Check the wiki, steam guides, and discussions to find some very helpful tips and information that the game couldn't explain to you.\n\nIt's middling between mediocre and fun at times. What makes it fun is upgrading your ship with new modules, unlocking special abilities, hoarding materials, researching higher tiers of items, modules, and clones. What would've made it great was if there were upgrades or QOL changes to make handling the more mediocre tasks less tedious. Crew with commander rank can handle spore cleanup, but it only happens when they randomly come across some.\n\nWhat makes it mediocre is the waiting around as you slowly strip a system of resources. At this time, after setting your crew to harvesting, you're just periodically patrolling the hangar, deposits, refinery, and access tunnels looking for alien stowaways and spores before selecting the next planet or debris field to harvest. Spores can cause diseases, some that are benign and others that aren't, and the alien stowaways can spread spores.\n\nYour upgrades will all come from 'sites' that are strewn across the many systems, but they can give really anything. Some basic weapon schematics, resource locations, suit chips, wormholes (fast travel points, you need at least two), and locations of alien species.\n\nThe crew is grown through cloning, after some RNG, you can grow different spliced clones provided you have the space and grown the associated plant for their species.\n\nThe game is structured like a rogue-like where once every crew has died, that run is over. But if you play well, a run can last as long as you bother to. If you've accrued some items, 'sites', and completed some achievements, you can have an even better/smoother start next time. After your tutorial run, you can select different corporations, which all have modules that are locked to that corporation, and they have access to more modules right off the bat that you'd otherwise have to farm sites for. For example, the tutorial corporation only has 20 slot storages, deposits, and a 2-man refinery, but the Stahl Union has a 48 slot deposit, a 3-man refinery, and your crew armors will look more industrial. I unlocked the Stahl Union tier II start, by refining 60 iron in my tutorial round.\n\nWhat QOL features are missing? Well, you can't assign your crew to different modules or give them work orders from just anywhere on the ship. For example, if I put my workshop on the top floor (floor 3) and need to assign someone to it or place a work order, and I'm on floor '-3', I have to get into my elevator and go all the way to floor 3  (the transition between floors is instant) and manually assign and place the order on the module's terminal. You can build some pretty big ships with enough time, so you're going to plan to keep modules like the tractor beam, hangar, deposits, refinery, and workshop together on the same floor, so you can quickly shift crew around when needed. \n\nSome nuance is that you can unassign crew from any module by using whatever module terminal nearest you, but if you need to place them back into their last module, you have to go to that module's terminal to put them back. Eventually you can unlock beam stations, so you can teleport and traverse much quicker.",
